  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nA slow-moving cold front has sagged into the region early this \nafternoon...with 18z/1pm analysis showing the boundary extending\nfrom southern Lake Michigan to just north of Kansas City. To the\nsouth of the front, record warm temperatures in the upper 80s\nand lower 90s prevail across south-central Illinois into much of\nMissouri. Meanwhile to the north, readings have dropped into the\n60s across central/northern Iowa. Aside from isolated convection\non the cool side of the boundary in western Iowa, the regional\nradar mosaic is presently mostly blank. This will change after\ndark as a 30-40kt 850mb jet streak oriented from Texas to Kansas\ntriggers widespread convection near the front across eastern \nKansas into northern Missouri/southern Iowa. This activity will \ngradually spread E/NE into parts of central Illinois late this \nevening into the overnight hours, bringing the risk for isolated\ndamaging wind gusts and localized downpours. The CAMs have \ngenerally been trending slower with convective arrival east of \nthe Mississippi River, so the primary time frame for a couple \nstrong storms will be from about 9pm through 3am. After that, \ninstability will wane and the scattered storms will gradually \nweaken toward sunrise.\n\nThe frontal boundary will drop further south on Wednesday,\nfocusing the most widespread showers/storms along and south of\nI-70 during the morning and early afternoon. After that, rain\nchances will steadily decrease from north to south across the\narea as the front sinks south of I-70. High temperatures on\nWednesday will be considerably cooler than today, but still\nabove normal for this time of year...ranging from around 70\ndegrees from the Peoria area northward to the lower 80s south of\nI-70.\n\nAfter a lull in precip chances Wednesday evening, a short-wave\ntrough interacting with the tail end of the departing front will\nbring an increased wave of convection late Wednesday night into\nThursday morning. While parameters do not support severe weather\nat that time, it appears this will be the wettest period of the\nupcoming forecast with widespread showers and a few thunderstorms\nlingering through midday Thursday. After that, we will need to \nkeep a close eye on radar and instability trends, as models \nsuggest the front will lift north of the region and showers \nwill become much less numerous. If partial clearing and \nsufficient destabilization can occur in the warm sector Thursday\nafternoon/evening, strong 0-6km bulk shear of 40-50kt may \nprovide the fuel for strong to severe thunderstorms along an \nadvancing cold front. CAMs that extend to that particular time \nframe such as the 3km NAM and RRFS are not particularly robust \nwith storm development, but think this may be underdone given \nthe strength of the shear and the approaching front. SPC \ncurrently highlights locations along/south of I-72 with a \nMarginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for severe. Stay tuned to later \nforecasts for additional details.\n\nOnce the cold front passes, a much cooler airmass will arrive by\nthe end of the week. High temperatures will drop below normal\ninto the upper 50s/lower 60s for both Friday and Saturday before\nrebounding nicely back into the 70s by next Monday/Tuesday. The\ncoolest night of the forecast period will be Friday night when\n12z Oct 13 models show surface ridging overhead...leading to\nexcellent radiational cooling and the potential for frost. The\n00z Oct 13 LREF shows a 5-15% chance of lows dipping below 32F\nalong/north of I-72. Temps will be a few degrees warmer \nSaturday night as the high moves east and southerly return flow \ncommences:however, there is a 5-10% chance of lows below 32F \neast of I-57.",
